public class PrimitiveTypesDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // 8 primitive types with fixed, platform-independent sizes
        byte b = 100;              // 8-bit signed integer
        short s = 32000;           // 16-bit signed integer
        int i = 2_000_000_000;     // 32-bit signed integer
        long l = 9_000_000_000L;   // 64-bit signed integer (L suffix)
        float f = 3.14f;           // 32-bit IEEE 754 float (f suffix)
        double d = 3.141592653;    // 64-bit IEEE 754 double
        char c = 'A';              // 16-bit Unicode character
        boolean flag = true;       // true or false

        // Autoboxing: primitive -> wrapper object
        Integer wrapped = Integer.valueOf(i);  // explicit
        Integer auto = i;                         // autoboxing
        int unboxed = auto;                        // unboxing

        // Widening conversion is implicit and safe
        double result = i + d;  // int promoted to double
        System.out.println("Result: " + result);

        // Narrowing requires explicit cast (may lose data)
        int narrowed = (int) 3.99;  // truncates to 3
        System.out.println("Narrowed: " + narrowed);

        // Integer cache: -128 to 127 are pre-allocated
        Integer x = 127, y = 127;
        System.out.println(x == y);  // true -- cached
        Integer big1 = 200, big2 = 200;
        System.out.println(big1 == big2);  // false -- not cached
    }
}

```java
public class PrimitivesDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        byte age = 25;                // small whole number
        short year = 2024;            // medium whole number
        int score = 95;               // default integer
        long userId = 9876543210L;    // large number — needs L
        float rating = 4.5f;          // decimal — needs f
        double price = 9.99;          // default decimal
        char grade = 'A';             // single character, 2 bytes
        boolean isPassed = true;      // true or false only

        System.out.println("Score: " + score + ", Grade: " + grade);
        System.out.println("User " + userId + " paid " + price);
    }
}
// Output:
// Score: 95, Grade: A
// User 9876543210 paid 9.99
```
Every variable here is a primitive — no `new` keyword, no objects, just values stored directly.

Put all three side by side and the difference is obvious — only the local needs an explicit value before use.

```java
class Counter {
    static int total;     // static: auto-initialized to 0
    int perObject;        // instance: auto-initialized to 0
    void process() {
        int count = 0;    // local: must initialize explicitly
        count++; total++; perObject++;
        System.out.println(count); // works
    }
}
```

`total` and `perObject` compile fine even untouched because the JVM zeroes fields when it allocates the object (or loads the class). `count` is your responsibility because the stack frame holds whatever was there before — so Java makes you set it.

Passing a primitive into a method is just another assignment — the parameter gets a copy. So a method can never change the caller's variable through the argument alone.

```java
public class ValueCopyDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int x = 100;
        modify(x);
        System.out.println(x); // still 100 — method got a copy
    }
    static void modify(int value) {
        value = 999; // only the local copy changes
    }
}
```

This is why a method that needs to hand a new value back must `return` it. Java is always pass-by-value — even for objects, what gets copied is the reference, not the object itself.

To make the contents un-changeable too, the object itself has to refuse mutation. `List.of(...)` returns exactly such a list.

```java
// The object itself must be unmodifiable for real immutability
final java.util.List<String> names = java.util.List.of("Alice", "Bob");
// names.add("Eve"); // UnsupportedOperationException — contents are locked too
```

These are two independent guarantees: `final` locks the binding so `names` always points at the same list, and `List.of(...)` (or a class with no setters and final fields) locks the contents so the list can't be edited. You usually want both.

This puts every flavour of `final` in one class so you can see the rules side by side — a constant set at declaration, a field set once in the constructor, a locked local, and a locked reference whose object still mutates.

```java
public class FinalDemo {
    private final String company = "TechCorp"; // set at declaration
    private final int foundedYear;
    public FinalDemo(int year) { this.foundedYear = year; } // set once in constructor

    void demo() {
        final int maxRetries = 3;
        // maxRetries = 5; // COMPILE ERROR
        final var names = new java.util.ArrayList<String>();
        names.add("Alice"); // OK — mutating the list is allowed
        System.out.println(company + " " + foundedYear + " " + names);
    }
}
```

Notice `foundedYear` has no value at its declaration — that's legal only because the constructor assigns it on every path. Miss one path and the compiler rejects the class outright.

The commented lines mark every spot `var` is rejected; the live lines show where it genuinely earns its place — verbose generics and for-each loops.

```java
import java.util.*;
public class VarExample {
    // var count = 0; // ERROR: not allowed on fields
    void process(List<Order> orders) {
        var byStatus = new HashMap<OrderStatus, List<Order>>(); // clear, concise
        for (var order : orders) {                              // works in for-each
            System.out.println(order);
        }
    }
    // public var getCount() { return 0; } // ERROR: not allowed as return type
}
enum OrderStatus { NEW, SHIPPED }
record Order(int id) {}
```

The `byStatus` declaration is where `var` pays off most — repeating `HashMap<OrderStatus, List<Order>>` on both sides adds nothing but noise. In the loop, the element type is obvious from `orders`, so `var order` stays perfectly readable.

This is the everyday reason wrappers and autoboxing exist: collections can only hold objects, so the `int` literals you add are boxed on the way in and unboxed on the way out — all without you writing a single conversion.

```java
import java.util.*;
public class BoxingDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Integer> scores = new ArrayList<>();
        scores.add(95);          // autobox: int 95 -> Integer.valueOf(95)
        scores.add(80);          // autobox again
        int first = scores.get(0); // unbox: Integer -> int
        int sum = scores.get(0) + scores.get(1); // unbox both, then add
        System.out.println("First: " + first + ", Sum: " + sum);
    }
}
// Output:
// First: 95, Sum: 175
```

Notice `List<Integer>` — never `List<int>`. Every `add(95)` boxes the primitive into an object, and every `get` unboxes it back. Convenient, but in a tight loop that hidden boxing allocates a fresh object each time, which is why performance-sensitive code prefers a primitive `int[]` over a `List<Integer>`.
